## How bad, exactly

Turpin et al.<Cite id="turpin" /> reordered few-shot examples so the right answer was always (A). Models followed the bias and never mentioned it. In the reward-hacking version<Cite id="chen" />, models exploited a planted wrong hint on more than 99% of examples and admitted it under 2% of the time, writing long justifications for the wrong answer instead. Longer explanations were less faithful than short ones, and harder questions made everything worse.

Real introspection exists, barely. Anthropic injected known concepts into model activations<Cite id="lindsey" />; Claude Opus 4.1 noticed and named them about 20% of the time, with zero false positives, before anything showed up in its output. The most capable models did best, so Fable 5 is plausibly the best introspector shipped. Nobody has published numbers for it, and from the outside a true report reads exactly like a confabulation.

So no, it won't lie. Lying needs access to the truth. It confabulates in good faith, and the good faith is what makes you believe it.

